    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 281100\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 AM MST Fri Aug 28 2026\n\n.UPDATE...12Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect through Saturday with\n  areas of Major to Extreme Heat Risk, despite temperatures \n  slowly decreasing.\n\n- Today will bring low end chances for showers and isolated \n  thunderstorms with increasing chances beginning Saturday.\n\n- An approaching low pressure system will bring much better\n  chances for rain, including a chance of strong to severe storms\n  across Southern Arizona mainly on Sunday and Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nCurrent objective analysis shows the subtropical high center along\nthe Arizona/New Mexico state line while a slug of decent mid and\nupper level moisture streams northeastward through California into\nthe Great Basin. Some of this moisture did make its way into\nArizona earlier on Thursday, but a pocket of drier air is already\nmoving into the state from the south around the high center. The\nridge is also in the first stages of weakening while slowly\nstarting to shift farther to the east. The drier air should limit\nconvective potential today, but CAMs are at least showing some\npotential focused over the eastern Arizona high terrain and over\nsoutheast California. Overall, we are not anticipating much with\nthis activity, but given the dry boundary layer we can't rule out\nsome strong gusty winds with any modest thunderstorms that may\ndevelop. This wind threat will mostly cover Imperial County where\nthe highest instability is expected. Temperatures today are\nforecast to be very similar to yesterday's highs of 110-114 \ndegrees, but clouds may hold temperatures a couple degrees cooler\nin some areas. \n\nAs the ridge shifts more into New Mexico and Texas tonight into \nSaturday, it will be the start of a significant pattern shift. A \nlarge Pacific trough off the Northwest U.S. coast will get \nreinforced and slide farther to the south and eventually to the \neast. During this transition this weekend, our region will see \nmodest to strong moisture advection with low level mixing ratios \ngradually improving from the current 7-8 g/kg to 10-11 g/kg late \nSaturday before peaking on Sunday and Monday at a respectable \n11-12 g/kg. Guidance suggests some potential for elevated showers \nand weak thunderstorms Friday night with the first big push of \nmoisture. As of now, the best chance for this rain should be from \nPhoenix to just to the east of Phoenix. \n\nWhat occurs Saturday afternoon/evening is still quite uncertain as\nit will be more of a conditional type environment for storm\ndevelopment. Moisture is expected to have increased enough to \nbring MUCAPEs up to around 1000 J/kg into south-central Arizona, \nbut not enough to erase all of the CIN. The question is where \nexactly will showers and storms develop Saturday afternoon and if \na large enough cluster can develop to force a strong or multiple \noutflows into the lower deserts. It is looking more encouraging \nfor storm potential as the amount of CIN does not look too high \nand any decent outflow should overcome it. We will also start to \nsee some upper level support from the incoming trough. The latest \n06Z HRRR hints at a cluster of storms firing across Pima County \nlate Saturday afternoon and then progressing northward or at least\nadditional storms developing along a northward progressing \noutflow during the evening. With a high DCAPE environment \nexpected, strong gusty winds are very possible across Maricopa and\nPinal County Saturday evening. Saturday should also be the last \nof the extreme heat with forecast highs dropping to 107-111 \ndegrees before a much more noticeable cool down begins Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nSunday and/or Monday are still looking quite interesting for\nthunderstorm potential with the environment potentially supporting\nsome severe storms. Some ingredients for strong to severe storms \nare expected to be present, like upper level support and \nsufficient instability, but wind shear will be somewhat lacking. \nMoisture is forecast to improve enough to support a nearly \nuncapped environment across south-central Arizona Sunday and \npotentially again Monday afternoon with MUCAPEs as high as\n1500-1800 J/kg. Both days have the potential to bring scattered \nto numerous showers and thunderstorms across south-central and \neastern Arizona. Strong to localized severe wind gusts, blowing \ndust, heavy rainfall, and flooding all look possible, but as \nstated above the threat may be localized due to the fairly weak \nshear which is oftentimes needed to sustain strong storms or\nclusters of storms. We should have a better idea on how this \nstrong to severe storm threat may evolve by Saturday/Saturday \nnight. Guidance then suggests some drying will occur by Tuesday \nwhich should decrease rain chances substantially through the \nmiddle of next week. \n\nTemperatures will drop into or even just below normal early next\nweek, but highs are still forecast to top 100 degrees each day\nacross the lower deserts. Once the drier air moves into the region\nby the middle of next week, we expect temperatures to quickly warm\nto a few degrees above normal but with little chance of reaching\n110 degrees again. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1053Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected through the TAF period. \nModels are still hinting at a small chance of VCSH, and potentially\na very isolated VCTS, popping up during the overnight into the \nearly morning hours tonight into Saturday morning (06-12Z), but \nany impacts should be minimal. There also continues to be signs of\nan outflow moving through the region late this evening, but \ntiming seems to be very brief and uncertain, otherwise typical \ndiurnal trends will continue. At most SCT-BKN medium to high \ncloud decks are expected through the period. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected through the TAF period. \nWinds will follow familiar diurnal trends with periods of VRB \nconditions, especially into the mid-morning hours. VCTS may be \npresent around KIPL by the afternoon, but forecast uncertainty \nremains regarding timing, therefore, a PROB30 remains in the TAF.\nIf convection does come to fruition, gusty outflow winds, and \nreduced VIS due to HZ/BLDU will be the main impacts. KBLH may see \nan outflow from distant TS activity move through, but confidence\nremains too low for the terminal for inclusion in the TAF at this\ntime. Mostly FEW-SCT high cloud decks will persist with periods of\nBKN. \n\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot and relatively dry conditions will continue into this weekend,\nbut with slight chances for isolated showers and thunderstorms. \nChances then increase considerably by Sunday, especially across \nSouthern and Central Arizona. Afternoon minimum humidities will \nimprove slightly to 15-20% today and then 17-25% Saturday. Winds \nwill be relatively light and follow diurnal tendencies today \nbefore south to southwesterly winds increase Saturday, \nparticularly across the western deserts, where afternoon gusts \nincrease up to 20-30 mph. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Saturday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST this evening for AZZ556-561-\n     562.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Saturday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT this evening for CAZ561-563-\n     564-568.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Ryan/RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
